---
/**
 * String Kit tool page.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import StringKit from '../../components/tools/StringKit';
---

<ToolLayout slug="string-kit">
  <StringKit client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Converting a web page to Markdown sounds like a formatting job and is really a judgement call.
      Raw HTML off a real site is mostly not the article: it is navigation, a cookie banner, a share
      bar, a newsletter box, a related-posts grid, and four scripts. A faithful converter renders
      all of it faithfully, and the result is unusable.
    </p>
    <p>
      The transforms below have a different problem. Each is a few lines and each has a version that
      looks right and is quietly wrong. <code>btoa(text)</code> throws on the first accented character.
      A camelCase splitter turns <code>parseHTTPResponse</code> into six words. A base64 decoder that
      does not accept the URL-safe alphabet fails on every JWT. These are the bugs that reach production,
      because the happy path is the one anybody tests.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      The converter works against the DOM rather than against a regular expression. The browser
      already has a spec-compliant HTML parser, and it is the same one that decides what the markup
      means: malformed tables, unclosed paragraphs, implied <code>tbody</code> and the full entity table
      all come out right for free. <code>DOMParser</code> builds an inert tree where scripts do not run,
      which is what makes it safe to point at a stranger&rsquo;s HTML.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of the work is deciding what to keep. Semantic tags are trusted first, because a page
      that says <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> has already answered the question. Failing that, a density
      score in the spirit of Readability: text outside links counts, link text does not, and a container
      that is more than half links is a menu no matter how much text it holds. Commas are the cheap prose
      signal, and they hold up, because a card grid has almost none and a written paragraph has many.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything discarded is listed under the output. A converter that silently drops half a page
      is worse than one that keeps too much, because you cannot tell which happened.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the transforms, each entry says where it breaks in the panel above the result. The
      round-trip check is the other half of that: where a transform has an exact inverse, the tool
      runs it and reports whether the result comes back byte-identical, on your input rather than on
      a test vector.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Content extraction is a heuristic.</strong> It gets a normal article right and can pick
        the wrong container on a page that is genuinely a link list, or on a layout that wraps the article
        in something the class-name check reads as chrome. Turn it off to convert everything and compare.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Some HTML has no Markdown.</strong> A table with merged cells cannot be a pipe table,
        so it degrades to a labelled list with a comment saying why. Superscripts, subscripts and collapsible
        sections are left as HTML, because flattening them changes what they mean.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>JavaScript-rendered pages give you the shell.</strong> This parses the HTML you paste.
        If the article is assembled by a framework after load, view-source has none of it, and you want
        the rendered DOM from your browser&rsquo;s inspector instead.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sentence splitting cannot see abbreviations.</strong> &ldquo;Dr.&rdquo; and &ldquo;e.g.&rdquo;
        start a new sentence as far as a regex is concerned. Noted on the transform rather than worked
        around, because the fix is a model and this is a text box.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Slugs drop what has no ASCII form.</strong> Decomposition turns café into cafe; Chinese,
        Arabic and Cyrillic have no such fallback and disappear. The character count shows it happening.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
